## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a practical, beginner-friendly CKNE-aware scheduler, a core building block for tenancy and backpressure.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE health per tenant
- Minute-by-minute adaptive scheduling
- Degradation policy and aging
- Payload schemas and end-to-end flow

## Code Example

```javascript
function pickNextJob(jobs, health, cap) {
  const grouped = {};
  for (const j of jobs) {
    const t = j.tenantId;
    grouped[t] = grouped[t] || { list: [], h: health[t] ?? 0 };
    grouped[t].list.push(j);
  }
  const entries = Object.entries(grouped).map(([t, g]) => {
    const prio = g.list[0].priority;
    const w = prio * (1 - (g.h / 100));
    return { t, w, list: g.list };
  });
  entries.sort((a,b)=> b.w - a.w);
  const chosen = entries.find(e => e.list.length > 0);
  return chosen?.list[0] ?? null;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you prevent starvation for tenants with chronic high ckne?
- How would you test with burst traffic and verify SLA adherence?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess how CKNE scores translate to practical controls in an API gateway, including per-tenant concurrency, circuit-breakers, and retry budgets, plus a simple scheduling loop and test plan.

## Key Concepts

- CKNE-to-resource mapping
- Fairness and SLA preservation
- Lightweight telemetry and safe preemption

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo minute loop
function ckneMinuteLoop(state){
  for (const t of state.tenants){
    if (t.ckne < 0.5) { t.concurrency = Math.max(1, t.concurrency-1); }
    else { t.concurrency = Math.min(state.maxConcurrency, t.concurrency+1); }
    t.retryBudget = t.ckne > 0.7 ? t.retryBudget : 1;
  }
}
```

